# The refusal bank: what `axiom fmt` does to each tests/fmt/parity/*.axp case.
#
# One `### <case> (exit N)` line per case, followed by the formatted bytes when
# it formatted at all. 50 cases: 25 rewrites and 25 refusals, which is why this
# file can tell a formatter that always succeeds from one that always refuses -
# check-fmt-selfhost.sh requires both outcomes to be present.
#
# TWO CASES WERE ADDED for the `region` keyword (2026-09-03, S2 of
# docs/memory-model-v2-design.md). 210 pins `fpRegion`'s head layout - the name
# on the head line, the one body indented under it, which is `while`'s layout
# and not the application layout a two-operand unknown head would get. 211 is
# the REFUSAL: `(region r)` has no body, `fpRegion` answers `fpBad` for any
# element count but two, and `check` refuses the same form at the parser
# (AX2001). Regenerating this file for them moved no other case's bytes.
#
# TWO CASES CHANGED SIDES on 2026-08-28, the other way round from the three
# below, and for the same reason - this file records what the formatter DID,
# not what the language allows:
#
#   070-keyword-in-expr     exit 1 -> 0   `(fn (f) (g data))`
#   131-effect-alloc-kw     exit 1 -> 0   `(fn (f n) (handle n (alloc) 0))`
#
# Both refusals came from `fpIsReservedWord`, the retired Rust compiler's lexer
# rule - every structural word reserved in every position - while the
# self-hosted parser reserves a keyword only in the position its rule claims
# (docs/reference.md, "Keywords"): `check` on the first complains only that `g`
# is unbound (AX3001, the checker), and answers OK to the second as written,
# because the checker accepts a lowercase `alloc` effect. check-fmt.sh met the
# rule the day `self_host/lsp.ax` named a parameter `data` (f0a2fa3). 190-197
# pin the rule in both directions: 190-193 and 195 are keyword-spelled
# identifiers that now print; 194 (`mut` at a binding head, the marker), 196
# (`consume`) and 197 (`begin`) are the refusals the parser shares - and 196
# and 197 were REWRITES before, output `check` refuses with AX2004.
#
# THREE CASES CHANGED SIDES on 2026-08-09, and the reason is worth recording
# here because this file is what recorded the wrong answer:
#
#   100-empty-form-dropped  exit 0 -> 1   `()` then `(fn (f) 1)`
#   170-empty-tuple         exit 0 -> 1   `(fn (f) ())`
#   172-empty-brace-body    exit 0 -> 1   `(fn (f) {})`
#
# 170 and 172 are the two programs that killed the compiler. `check` on either
# of them was SIGSEGV - exit 139, no output - and this bank recorded, as the
# correct answer, that the formatter rewrites them and exits 0. It could not
# see the crash because it never asked the compiler anything: it compares the
# formatter with its own previous output. 100 is the other direction and needs
# no crash to be wrong: `()` at the top level is `AX2003` from this compiler,
# the formatter DELETED the form and exited 0, so `axiom fmt` turned a file the
# compiler refuses into one it accepts. The gate now asks the compiler about
# every case as well (section 4), and refuses to read a signal as silence
# (section 5b).
#
# Materialized from the Rust compiler at commit 1041f11 on 2026-08-08, and
# verified at that commit to be byte-identical to what the self-hosted compiler
# produces on all 37 cases - status and bytes. That is the provenance, recorded
# on the last day there were two compilers to compare. It replaces the
# stage0-vs-stage1 comparison this bank used to be read by, which would have
# gone silently green the moment both sides became the same binary.
#
# `.axp`, not `.ax`, so check-fmt.sh's every-file-formats sweep does not trip
# over the deliberate refusals.
#
# Regenerate deliberately, never automatically:
#
#   axc=$(mktemp -d)/axc
#   .axiom-bin/axiom build --input self_host/main.ax --output "$axc"
#   t=$(mktemp -d)
#   for f in tests/fmt/parity/*.axp; do
#     cp "$f" "$t/case.ax"; "$axc" fmt "$t/case.ax" >/dev/null 2>&1; s=$?
#     echo "### $(basename "$f") (exit $s)"
#     [ $s -eq 0 ] && cat "$t/case.ax"
#   done
#
# and keep this header. A re-bless satisfies this file and nothing else: the
# gate's preservation check reads these same cases against their own source
# bytes, and the rebuild half never reads a golden at all.
